  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n- Periods of rain through tonight, locally heavy at times\n\nThe rain event we have been focusing on for the last few days is now \nunderway. A long line of showers here, extends all of the way back \ninto Northern Iowa where storms are present. This line is the edge \nof the elevated warm front where the low level jet is surging deep \nmoisture over it. Precipitable waters pushing 2 inches is at the \ncore of this moist air mass advecting in.\n\nThe rain here so far has been on lighter side with limited low level \nmoisture, and with the best forcing focused west of the area so far. \nThis rain is at the leading edge of the low level jet, and we will \nsee the more concentrated rain push in between now and sunrise.\n\nThere is plenty of thunder to our west, but we are not expecting \nthat to be as widespread as it is currently. Instability progs like \nMU CAPE show the elevated instability gradient staying mostly SW of \nthe forecast area, except the far SW corner of the area. Severe \nweather potential is quite low, however this area is most likely to \nsee the heavier convective bursts under the stronger updrafts and \nhighest pwats. We could end up needing some Flood Advisories with the \nheavier bursts. This first batch should be pushing out of the area \nby noon.\n\nWe will see the intensity of the rain diminish, but we do not expect \na complete break in the rain for the afternoon. The models are \nindicating that some semblance of a low level jet will remain, just \nnot as strong of a core as this morning. This overrunning will \ncontinue, and keep rain possible, even if it is just drizzle with \nlower moisture becoming plentiful.\n\nWe expect the rain to increase in coverage and intensity once again \ntoward and after sunset as another low level jet core increases \nahead of the main sfc low. This lasts into the early overnight hours \nbefore the elevated front/baroclinic zone, and low level jet move \nsouth of the area. Again, not expecting much thunder with elevated \ninstability fairly weak. Heavy rain is once again the main threat, \nespecially after the rain from earlier priming the ground and \nrivers/streams. The highest potential for this is where the Flood \nWatch is across the SW corner of the forecast area. River forecast \npoints will also see good increases.\n\nThere has been some uncertainty whether Sunday sees residual rain or \nnot. It looks like the best chance of any rain beyond sunrise on \nSunday would be early, and would move out quickly as the front sinks \nsouth of the area. Clouds are likely to hold in most of the day.\n\n- Cooler period through the week with small chances of rain\n\nThis wet system for this weekend will move out, but we are not \nlooking at any extended dry period behind it. We will see a weak \nshort wave ridge pass overhead on Monday, which may keep all of the \narea dry. \n\nWe quickly see an upper low complex over the Upper Midwest rotate \nover the area, and merge with additional upper level energy to \nbecome a bigger complex. This complex then looks to sit over the \narea from Tuesday through the end of the week.\n\nTiming and exact track of any weak short waves rotating around it \nwill be problematic as is the case always with upper lows. What we \ndo know is that is will keep a cooler air mass over the area, likely \nkeeping temperatures in the 60s. Timing of showers will be a little \nmore favorable in the afternoon hours when some diurnal instability \ncan help with the development. This looks to hold pretty much \nthrough Friday, before the larger upper ridge can push this system \neast of the area.",
